meta {
  name: users forbidden
  type: http
  seq: 6
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/users
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{memberAccessToken}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "email": "should-not-be-created@example.com",
    "name": "Should Not Exist",
    "password": "correct horse battery staple"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 403
  res.body.error.code: eq FORBIDDEN
}

docs {
  403, not 404. The member is authenticated and the collection exists; what
  is missing is permission. Hiding it behind 404 would be lying to a client
  that has every right to be told it lacks access.

  Contrast with users-no-token.bru, which is 401: no identity at all.

  This is POST, not GET: read_only — the default role setup-member relies
  on, by omitting role entirely — holds user:list and user:read, so listing
  or reading with a member token now succeeds. user:create is exactly the
  gap that role does not close.
}
